[{:seon.task/name "a-merged-tree-is-analyzed-in-a-fork"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: first measure what refresh-tx in root catches that a member's own import and seon.merge/dangling do not; if nothing, delete unanalyzable instead; Optimization measures, numbers to the owner Sean before any merge-blocking check. From an independent audit of Context's merges (2026-11-08), approved by the owner. Done: a merge that leaves a name unbound (an octopus member calling what another removes, or one branch removing what main still calls) is refused from the recorded references, with no process (docs/merging.md#a-merge-leaves-no-name-unbound). Left: seon.writer/unanalyzable runs source/refresh-tx in root's own image, so before a merge is decided root evaluates the candidate's ns form (which can require), its defmacros, unaliases live namespaces and flips a live var's :private (seon.source/alias-held-not-declared guards one symptom). Learned 2026-11-08: a process loading the merged program from main's (seon.program/load-diff!, as an agent resumed on a branch does) does not see a removed name called as x/f, since Jolt compiles a name under an alias with no var as a lookup at each call; it does see an unqualified one and a changed macro's callers. A child that loads needs no new mechanism: an agent with no task spawned on a branch holding the candidate runs load-diff! at start and its hello carries :load-errors (root's set-agent would answer them with the spawn). Before building, measure what refresh-tx in root catches that a member's own import (analyzed in its own process when recorded) and seon.merge/dangling do not; if nothing seen, delete unanalyzable and alias-held-not-declared rather than move them. A merge-blocking check: its numbers go to the owner before it lands."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-main-notice-says-whether-program-content-moved"
  :seon.task/plan "Design 6a's real form (Optimization, measured 2026-11-10; notes writer-latency-2026-11-10.md and turn-split-real-estimates-2026-11-10.md). An agent's request through the server is a whole outside turn: 91-114 ms, 14-15 M ticks, 70-79 MB. About 3 M of it is main's content diff: main-base-tx's seon.merge/content-commit, plus the reply's main-difference/delta-changes. A model turn pays seon.merge/content-datoms at 1.8 M a call, 7-10 calls a turn (13.6-17.9 M of a 35-39 M turn). They run on every request because main moves on every request by facts that are no program: root writes :seon.agent/status per submit (busy, then idle), and on a fresh store the vector proc commits embeddings 64 a batch. A cheap skip (main-base-tx answering from its record when main is the very commit it recorded; jolt/mainbase, proved red-first) measured NO gain for that reason and was not landed. The cut: seon.writer/main-commit already holds each stored commit's resolved datoms (:ops). It marks on the :main notice whether any of them is program content (seon.merge's content attributes: forms, definitions, schema entries, config, sections), and the agent takes main's move with no content diff and no merge-main! when only bookkeeping moved it. The same notice serves main's four readers (render, vectors, prove, reconciler): each skips a commit whose ops touch nothing it reads (the 3-25 ms they add to every write when all wake: all four paused, a write 10-12 ms against 19-21; Point, 2026-11-10: one mechanism). Point ruled not to move :seon.agent/status off main: with the mark, a status write wakes nobody who does not read status. Estimate: -3 M ticks and -25-35 ms an agent request and a model turn while only bookkeeping moves main; content-datoms itself still to make one descent (design 6a's second half)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-main-notice-says-whether-program-content-moved seon.server-test/a-turn-on-a-main-moved-by-bookkeeping-makes-no-content-diff}}
]
